\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_map.pdf}
\caption{Study area. Blue dots are the 325 PEMS-BAY sensors, which trace the freeway network of the southern Bay Area. Stars are sports venues, sized by capacity; filled stars host events during the study window. SAP Center and Avaya Stadium sit inside the sensor network, while the Oakland and San Francisco venues lie well outside it, which motivates the distance-to-venue channel. Triangles are the ASOS weather stations.}
\label{fig:map}
\end{figure}

The event table holds 79 MLB, 34 NBA, 27 NHL, and 9 MLS events at AT\&T Park, Oakland Coliseum, Oracle Arena, Avaya Stadium, and SAP Center. Expected attendance ranges from 10,292 to 42,862 with a mean near 24,282. Figure~\ref{fig:timeline} shows when these events fall relative to the chronological split: every MLB game lies in April or later, so the validation and test periods are baseball-heavy while the training period is dominated by NBA and NHL games. Any conclusion about event-window performance has to keep this composition shift in mind.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig2_event_timeline.pdf}
\caption{All 149 scheduled events by venue (rows ordered north to south as in Figure~\ref{fig:map}), colored by league and sized by expected attendance. Dashed lines mark the chronological 70/10/20 split. The MLB season starts in April, so event types are distributed unevenly across the three subsets.}
\label{fig:timeline}
\end{figure*}

In the hourly weather table, temperature spans 0.7 to 39.0\,$^\circ$C, precipitation reaches 7.3\,mm per hour, and wind peaks at 37.5\,km/h. There are 686 hours with positive precipitation, 194 of them with at least 1\,mm. Figure~\ref{fig:weather} in the appendix shows the seasonal pattern: the wet weeks cluster in January and February, including the storm sequence around 20 February 2017, and rain becomes rare after April. Adverse-weather windows therefore concentrate in the training period, mirroring the event-side imbalance in the opposite direction.

Figure~\ref{fig:impact} in the appendix previews the phenomenon the model is supposed to capture. Around SAP Center, the sensors most affected by hockey games run about 4\,mph slower than the weekday-matched baseline right after the typical game end, and the spread across event days widens through the whole evening. The mean effect is modest; the variance is not. This is precisely the pattern that average test-set error hides.

\subsection{Preprocessing}

For the speed-only baseline, the adjacency matrix is built from the road-distance file with the threshold-ed Gaussian kernel, speeds are ordered by sensor identifier, small gaps are interpolated, and 12-to-12 windows are sliced. The 70/10/20 split happens before normalization; training batches are shuffled, but no sample crosses a split boundary. The PEMS-BAY time axis skips the daylight-saving gap on 12 March 2017, and weather and event records must respect the same axis. For the conditioned model, the remaining pipeline aligns weather and events to the 5-minute grid, adds calendar features, builds the venue-distance representation, assembles the $[52{,}116\times325\times9]$ tensor, and normalizes continuous channels with training statistics. \tbd{[TODO: document timezone handling, per-variable interpolation rules, event-window mapping with any pre/post buffers, overlap rules, and any info on data leakage]}

\subsection{Setup and baselines}

The baseline notebook ran with Python 3.13.15, PyTorch 2.11.0 with CUDA 12.8, and one NVIDIA A100 40\,GB GPU; final configurations use seeds 42, 43, and 44. Two trivial baselines frame the learned models: persistence repeats the last observed speed across the horizon, and the historical average predicts the mean speed for each sensor and calendar slot from training data alone. DCRNN remains optional and time-boxed; it enters the comparison only if a faithful run completes. \tbd{[TODO: implement both trivial baselines; report runtimes and selected epochs for all models.]}

\subsection{Metrics}

With $\mathcal{M}$ the evaluated prediction set, $y_i$ observed and $\hat y_i$ predicted speed, all after de-normalization:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{MAE}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}}\left|\hat y_i-y_i\right|,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{RMSE}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}}\left(\hat y_i-y_i\right)^{2}},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{MAPE}=\frac{100}{|\mathcal{M}'|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}'}\frac{\left|\hat y_i-y_i\right|}{\left|y_i\right|},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{M}'=\{i\in\mathcal{M}:|y_i|>1\text{ mph}\}$ masks near-zero speeds. Metrics are reported at steps 3, 6, and 12, i.e., 15, 30, and 60 minutes. \tbd{[TODO: add definitions for onset error, the sudden-event cross-check, and the decision cost once their implementations are frozen.]}

\subsection{Speed-only STGCN reproduction}

Table~\ref{tab:repro} compares our three-seed reproduction with the STGCN values that Wu et al.~\shortcite{wu2019graphwavenet} report for PEMS-BAY. The gap stays within 0.1\,mph MAE at every horizon, which we take as evidence that the loader, graph, and training loop are sound. These numbers say nothing yet about anomalous windows: the model sees only speed, and the metrics average over the full test set.

